    "rich_text_Djzh9q": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_zXDLVi": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "&nbsp;Utility & Cargo eBikes&nbsp;",
            "heading_size": "h1"
          }
        },
        "text_6xHekj": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<h2><strong>What Makes a Great Utility and Cargo eBike?</strong></h2><p>A great cargo e-bike does more than haul a backpack. It replaces short car trips. The best cargo bikes turn grocery runs, school drop-offs, gear hauls, and last-mile errands into quiet, low-cost rides that still feel like riding a bike.</p><p>Here is what separates a real cargo e-bike from a regular e-bike with a basket:</p><ul><li><strong>Real rear platform capacity.</strong> Both Kodiak models ship with a sturdy aluminum rear rack rated for up to <strong>120 lb</strong> of cargo.</li><li><strong>Total payload for everyday use.</strong> Rider plus cargo capacity is rated up to <strong>300 lb</strong>.</li><li><strong>Step-through frames.</strong> Easy on and off when you are loading bags, kids’ gear, or a second passenger setup.</li><li><strong>Power under load.</strong> Both bikes use a <strong>Bafang 500W (750W peak)</strong> hub motor with <strong>80 Nm</strong> of torque.</li><li><strong>Range for a full day of errands.</strong> Listed range is <strong>80–100+ km</strong> on the Kodiak and <strong>100+ km</strong> on the Kodiak V2 (real-world range varies with load, hills, assist level, and temperature).</li><li><strong>Street-ready stopping power.</strong> Hydraulic disc brakes front and rear (Tektro Auriga) with <strong>180 mm</strong> rotors.</li></ul>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_zXDLVi",
        "text_6xHekj"
      ],
